<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalayirid Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jalayirids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jalayirid Sultanate was a Mongol-descended dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the fourteenth century. It emerged from the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> after the death of Abu Sa'id in 1335. Its rise marks the dissolution of Mongol authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate.jpg" alt="Jalayirid Sultanate" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Demis Map Server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jalayirids,_Muzaffarids_in_1374.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Abu Saʿid died in 1335 without an heir, the Ilkhanate fragmented into rival successor states controlled by regional dynasties, including the <b>Jalayirids</b> and the Chupanids, who ruled through puppet khans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century they lived in the shadow of the <b>Jalayirid Sultanate</b> of Baghdad and Tabriz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jalayirids</b> presented themselves as heirs of the Ilkhans and patronised Persian culture: the poet Salman Savaji praised them, and Jalayirid manuscript painters, such as Junayd, shaped the later Timurid style.</blockquote><a href="/state/jalayirids">Jalayirids</a></li></ul></section>
